### 6.4 Which files should *not* ship in the public site

- Test runner scripts, test fixture credentials, source archives, SQL dumps, private settings or database backups.
- Private PHP modules or wallet service credentials.
- `INSTALL_PROOF` material, one-time setup payload, installer internals after completion.
- Source maps or logs that expose privileged flows, tokens or unnecessary customer information.

Some browser-side `vendor/*.js` dependencies are public assets by design. Do not indiscriminately block required browser libraries; determine public/private placement per file.

## 7. Requirements and environment preparation

| Dependency | Minimum checked by current v1.0 diagnostics | Recommended deployment guidance |
|---|---|---|
| PHP | 8.2+ | A currently supported, security-patched PHP release compatible with all dependencies; validate per-host |
| Web server | Apache or compatible shared-hosting configuration | HTTPS with controlled document root, no misrouted `.mjs` assets |
| Database | PDO MySQL/MariaDB | Dedicated least-privilege application access; versioned backups |
| Crypto extension | Sodium | Required for supported cryptographic operations; do not improvise crypto |
| HTTP client | cURL/OpenSSL | TLS peer/hostname checks on, outbound HTTPS enabled |
| ZIP extension | ZipArchive | Needed for installation/update-verification paths |
| Other PHP | JSON, PDO, filter/session support | Verify all features on the *web* PHP handler |
| Browser | Modern Chrome, Firefox, Safari or Edge | Secure context, Web Crypto, IndexedDB; test exact device |
| Test runner | CLI PHP and optional Node.js | Development-only, not necessarily installed on shared hosting |
| Hosting control | cPanel or DirectAdmin | File Manager, domain manager, SSL, phpMyAdmin and logs |

A cPanel host may expose multiple PHP versions; the terminal's `php` CLI version may differ from the selected web virtual host's version. Use cPanel **MultiPHP Manager** to verify the affected domain's version.

### 7.1 Local PHP inventory (read-only)

```bash
php -v
php -m
php -r 'foreach (["PDO", "pdo_mysql", "sodium", "curl", "zip", "openssl"] as $e) { echo $e, ": ", extension_loaded($e) ? "PASS" : "MISSING", PHP_EOL; }'
php -r 'echo "PHP_VERSION_ID=", PHP_VERSION_ID, PHP_EOL;'
node --version   # Optional: JavaScript tests only
```

No secrets are required for these commands. PHP extension names and availability can differ between distributions.

### 7.2 HTTP response checks

```bash
curl --head https://wallet.example.org/
curl --head https://wallet.example.org/assets/tenant-v090.mjs
```

An authenticated page may redirect to sign-in. A served `.mjs` module must use a suitable JavaScript MIME type. Never expose a general-purpose private diagnostics endpoint just to print the PHP configuration.
