- https://www.softaculous.com/docs/developers/making-custom-package/
- https://www.softaculous.com/docs/admin/adding-custom-scripts/
- https://www.softaculous.com/docs/api/api/

Do not copy insecure SSL-disable examples from third-party installer snippets; TLS peer and host validation must remain enabled.
## 14. Tenant registration and external dApp integration

### 14.1 Roles and responsibilities

| Role | Intended access |
|---|---|
| Platform administrator | Create/suspend tenants, inspect platform health, handle exceptional support actions |
| Tenant owner | Manage their organization's people, policies and applications |
| Tenant administrator | Manage permitted app settings without acquiring owner-only membership authority |
| Tenant viewer | Read permitted organization data only; no registration or policy mutation |
| dApp end user | Approve their own connection or transactions, not administer tenants |

Role enforcement must be server-side; hiding a button is not an access-control system. A platform administrator's membership in the default migrated tenant is separate from ownership of every customer wallet.

### 14.2 Correct example of a registered dApp

Suppose the wallet is at `https://wallet.example.org` and a merchant wants `demo3` on another hostname:

| Registration field | Correct example |
|---|---|
| Application name | `demo3` |
| Exact HTTPS origin | `https://demo3.example.org` |
| Exact callback URL | `https://demo3.example.org/callback.html` |
| Scope | `address.read` only for first connection test |
| Transaction request permission | Disabled initially; enable separately for tested spend flows |

The callback page must be served by the registered dApp itself. Register via the authenticated `tenants.php` form, **not** by navigating to the POST-only action URL in a browser.

### 14.3 Why a path is not an origin

These are **the same origin**:

```text
https://wallet.example.org/
https://wallet.example.org/demo/
https://wallet.example.org/demo/callback.html
```

These are **different origins**:

```text
https://wallet.example.org/
https://demo3.example.org/
```

Even different origins may share an underlying hosting folder when incorrectly mapped. Give the wallet and dApp distinct document roots and check in the hosting panel. Strict browser-origin separation is necessary but not sufficient for host security.

### 14.4 v0.9 Phase B R2 friendly validation

| Server or browser condition | Correct user guidance |
|---|---|
| Origin URL has a path (`/demo`) | "Enter the HTTPS origin only: https://hostname" |
| Callback lacks `https://`/host | "Enter a complete HTTPS callback URL" |
| Callback host differs from registered origin | "Callback must be on the dApp's exact registered origin" |
| dApp tries to use wallet origin | "Host the dApp on a separate HTTPS origin and document root" |
| `ORIGIN_ALREADY_ASSIGNED` (HTTP 409) | "This site origin is already registered to another organization" |
| `INVALID_DAPP_REGISTRATION` (HTTP 422) | "Correct application name, origin and callback" with field-specific error |
| `POST_REQUIRED` | "Submit the registration form; direct GET browsing cannot create apps" |
| App exists but is disabled | "Ask this tenant's authorized owner to review its status" |

The backend must continue returning an appropriate machine-readable code while the frontend shows humane text. Avoid disclosing which other organization owns an origin to unauthorized users.

### 14.5 Existing records and duplicate requests

Registration can succeed while an unrelated frontend reset/refresh fails. Before retrying after a UI error, reload the tenant list and check whether the application already exists. Avoid duplicate app/client registrations and distinguish "saved, refresh failed" from "API rejected". The R2 hotfix preserves the earlier null-form reset correction.

## 15. Authorization, SDK and mobile-browser workflows

### 15.1 SDK concepts

The existing Coxy Connect SDK was introduced for independent website-to-wallet communication. It accepts the wallet origin, registered public client identifier, full redirect callback and requested test network. The SDK facilitates a browser redirect/approval flow; it must not be treated as a shared secret or as proof that a payment cleared.

**Conceptual usage based on the reviewed Coxy Connect developer guide:**

```js
// The merchant hosts the approved, version-matched Coxy Connect module
// under its OWN origin. Import path may differ by the SDK package.
import { CoxyConnect } from '/assets/coxy-connect.mjs';

const wallet = new CoxyConnect({
  walletOrigin: 'https://wallet.example.org',
  clientId: 'REGISTERED_PUBLIC_CLIENT_ID',
  redirectUri: 'https://shop.example.org/wallet-callback.html',
  network: 'preprod'
});

// Trigger only from an explicit user action.
await wallet.connect();  // address.read flow

// On the exact registered callback page, using the same config:
// const account = await wallet.resume();
```

Confirm the exact API/export path with the SDK version installed on the site; this is a tutorial example, not a claim that the above placeholder client identifier is functional. The browser's public client ID may appear in source. Private merchant signing credentials, session tokens and wallet keys must not.

### 15.2 OAuth and PKCE verification

The authorization server must enforce exact redirect URI matching, a one-time authorization code, S256 PKCE, request-bound `state`/CSRF protections, short token lifetimes, restrictive scopes and immediate revocation upon app disable or tenant suspension. The callback cannot be an arbitrary URL. Validate the tenant and dApp at **both** authorization and token exchange, and for subsequent API use.

OAuth security details are documented in RFC 9700: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700.html

### 15.3 Permission semantics

- `address.read`: Public receiving/account address access as approved by the wallet user. Should not expose unrelated accounts or shielded state.
- `tx.request`: Application may ask for a transaction review. The wallet user still approves the exact chain, asset, recipient, amount, fees and details.
- Tenant permission and user consent are separate. One cannot replace the other.
- A consent record is bound to the registered origin/client, account, network, scopes and revocation state.

### 15.4 Mobile browser, popup and return-to-dApp

Mobile browsers often block unsolicited popups, have restricted background execution and do not offer the same extensions as desktop browsers. The best broadly compatible architecture is a **user-initiated top-level redirect to the dedicated wallet origin**, with a registered callback to the merchant site. Retain the order/authorization correlation on the merchant backend, not in an untrusted query parameter. Avoid depending on third-party cookies for this flow.

A robust mobile session should handle:

1. User clicks **Connect** or **Pay with Coxy Wallet** on the merchant.
2. Merchant creates a one-time intent and redirects to the dedicated wallet.
3. Wallet authenticates the user and presents a clear approval screen.
4. If signing is supported for that network, the browser vault/devices enforce their unlock and approval rules.
5. Wallet returns through the exact callback with an authorization result or transaction reference.
6. Merchant independently queries its backend/payment status; the callback does not prove settlement.
7. Interruptions, back button, expired sessions and reloads produce a safe recoverable state.

### 15.5 The Midnight exception

The v0.7.x Midnight panel supports network health and a **read-only browser connector** to a Midnight-compatible injected wallet such as Lace. It does not provide independent extension-free shielded-wallet management in ordinary phone browsers. Display `unavailable` when a wallet does not expose a private/shielded balance method; do not invent `0` for inaccessible private data. Full Midnight private wallet support is a distinct audited integration milestone.

## 16. Customer payment lifecycle and reconciliation

### 16.1 Payment intent creation

The merchant backend creates a unique order reference and an immutable payment intent containing, at minimum:

- Tenant, registered dApp, environment and network identity.
- Order ID, payment intent ID, currency/asset identification and exact integer quantity in native units.
- Recipient address or script policy, expiry and merchant authorization state.
- Amount and fee presentation policy, checksum/fingerprint and idempotency identifier.
- Allowed confirmation/finality criteria and the response/error state.

Never use floating-point JavaScript arithmetic as the canonical representation for satoshis, lamports, drops, wei or lovelace. Use checked arbitrary precision integers/decimal strings.

### 16.2 Transaction approval

For a network with tested signing capabilities, prepare the transaction using exact chain identity. Display the contract interaction or value transfer, recipient, asset amount, gas/network fee cap, and the implications of failure. Check the estimate against current network rules; the Sepolia R3 experience demonstrated why hardcoded 21,000-gas assumptions can fail. Require a fresh user approval for any meaningful change to transaction details.

No web administrator or tenant owner may automatically sign on behalf of a customer's device simply because a payment intent exists.

### 16.3 A state machine that preserves traceability

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> prepared
    prepared --> expired: timeout or cancel
    prepared --> approved: user consents
    approved --> submitting: signed broadcast attempted
    submitting --> submitted: hash observed
    submitting --> uncertain: provider times out
    submitted --> confirmed: validated chain receipt + policy depth
    submitted --> failed: definitive failure/revert
    uncertain --> submitted: later discovered by hash
    uncertain --> failed: definitive evidence of rejection
    confirmed --> [*]
    failed --> [*]
    expired --> [*]
```

Real implementations may use additional states, but the invariants are essential: do not silently reinterpret `uncertain` as `failed` and rebroadcast; keep a reliable payment/event history and an explicit reconciliation action. A transaction with a reverted receipt consumes fees even when the intended value was not transferred.

### 16.4 Acceptance criteria for a payment

The back end must compare the observed transaction against the signed intent: chain/network, recipient, currency, amount, transaction hash, execution status, confirmation depth and double-payment prevention. Never rely only on a wallet UI banner or a third-party redirect. For UTxO systems, consider outputs and transaction metadata, not only a one-address balance delta. Maintain a documented policy for chain reorganizations and rare provider disagreements.

### 16.5 Merchant integration with existing web frameworks

**WordPress/WooCommerce:** A plugin or payment gateway module should create intents server-side, redirect customers into the wallet, receive the callback and reconcile through a background job. WooCommerce order state should change only after verified settlement. Do not store customer keys in WordPress options.

**Laravel:** Use an authenticated server service class, queue workers for confirmation polling, strongly scoped merchant keys, idempotent DB transactions and signed/replay-safe webhooks. Do not let a browser set a Laravel order to paid directly.

**Plain PHP/cPanel LMS:** Use `coxy-connect.mjs` for user consent and a local PHP module for merchant payment intent/state. Store course enrollment/payment linkage under a unique order ID and record callbacks separately from reconciled payments.

**Non-PHP frameworks (Django, Rails, Node, .NET):** The website may use the SDK/browser redirection and a documented HTTP integration, subject to tested protocol/authentication support. The wallet server itself remains PHP/MySQL in the currently reviewed architecture.

### 16.6 Refunds and noncustodial limitations

A refund is generally a new authorized blockchain payment; it is not an SQL status change. The merchant needs its own custody/account policy, customer destination verification and traceable refund workflow. Chain fees and reversibility depend on the network and token. Do not promise card-style chargebacks for irreversible blockchain transfers.

## 17. Blockchain adapter reference

Each network must have its own adapter identity, address validation, native units, RPC allowlist and capability flags. The following describes the reviewed **v0.7.x+** integrated architecture, not a declaration that every chain has a working signer.

| Chain/environment | Core module status | Native unit | Current limitation or identity check |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cardano Preprod | Existing wallet/signing path; v0.8 assets/review extras | lovelace | v0.8 mint draft unsigned; contract simulation not complete |
| Ethereum Sepolia | R3 native test ETH send succeeded previously | wei | Gas estimate + fee policy + receipt validation |
| Base Sepolia | Existing EVM adapter path | wei | Re-test chain ID, fees and provider independently |
| Bitcoin Testnet4 | Read-only adapter in v0.7.x | satoshi | Signed Bitcoin transactions not added by read-only layer |
| Dogecoin Testnet | Read-only adapter; provider availability varied | koinu | Provider timeout/reliability checks |
| Solana Devnet | Read-only v0.7 adapter | lamport | RPC domain/path security; do not assume sending exists here |
| XRP Ledger Testnet | Read-only adapter | drop | Unfunded account is not activated; no signing in v0.7 |
| BNB Smart Chain Testnet | Read-only v0.7 adapter | wei | Chain ID 97 / `0x61`; Alchemy testnet path allowlist |
| Ethereum Classic Mordor | Read-only v0.7 adapter | wei | Chain ID 63 / `0x3f`; provider may reject unsupported methods |
| TRON Nile | Read-only adapter | sun | Not Shasta; new account can be unactivated |
| Midnight Preview/Preprod | Indexer health + user-approved read-only Lace connector | network/wallet-dependent | Shielded access depends on compatible wallet API; not general extension-free signing |

Other earlier Coxy Multichain v4.x experiments may have broader signing implementations, such as TRON Shasta and ETC. **Those are a separate code line**. Their feature claims do not automatically carry into the current Coxy Wallet Engine v0.7.x overlay. Avoid copying Shasta RPC configuration into Nile or an ETC Mainnet provider into Mordor.

### 17.1 RPC provider failure handling

Return a specific, sanitized error for: URL policy rejection, wrong testnet identity, HTTP 401/403, HTTP 404, HTTP 429, HTTP 5xx, DNS failure, TLS failure, timeout, malformed JSON and invalid balance format. Only allow operators to configure provider URLs in the protected backend. Never let an end-user input a server-side arbitrary RPC URL, never disable TLS checks and never reveal API keys in browser diagnostics.

### 17.2 Adapter checklist for a new chain

- Network ID/genesis or chain ID, native unit and display precision.
- Public address validation with canonical encoding/checksum.
- Provider host/path allowlist, DNS/IP safety and rate limits.
- Bounded HTTP timeout and response size; strict schema parsing.
- Health probe that does not require an address, plus known-address read.
- Offline parsers with negative, overflow, empty/unfunded and wrong-network tests.
- Explicit signing capability flags, initially `disabled`.
- Explorer links, transaction status handling and provider disagreement logic.
- Administrator diagnostics, audit and a per-chain emergency kill switch.

## 18. Administrator diagnostics and health checks

| Endpoint/file | What a PASS establishes | What it does NOT establish |
|---|---|---|
| `diagnostic-v070.php` | Synthetic adapter correctness and optional live provider reads | User-owned funded balance or transaction success without separate testing |
| `diagnostic-upgrade.php` | PHP extension, private-path, installer-lock, v0.8/v0.9/v1.0 prerequisites | Production audit or complete installer integration |
| `diagnostic-tenant-v090.php` | Schema, origin mapping, default owner integrity | Unbypassable runtime ACLs |
| `diagnostic-phase-b-v090.php` | Tenant gate hooks present, staging flag state | Correct cross-tenant authorization under real load |
| `Coxy_Wallet_install_path_preflight.php` | CLI-only installation path/lock/bootstrap checks | A web page; opening directly intentionally returns HTTP 404 |
| `api.php?action=addon_health` (where available) | Authenticated application health/version hints | Guarantees about key security or Mainnet readiness |

### 18.1 Normal Phase B diagnostic progression

```text
SCHEMA_AND_MAPPING_READY
          |
READY_TO_ACTIVATE_ON_STAGING
          |
ENABLED_LIVE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED
          |
     Manual two-tenant and payment security tests
          |
     Independent security review and release acceptance
```

A static `PRESENT` status means the code hook is present; it is *not* an end-to-end access control test. Do not convert `ENABLED_LIVE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED` to green merely by changing a display constant.

### 18.2 Safer browser diagnostics for users without SSH

For the future v1.0 installer, provide a strictly authenticated browser-accessible administrator dashboard with only sanitized status codes: `private_path_ok`, `install_locked`, `schema_version`, `network_reachable`, `origin_mode`, `mainnet_disabled`, and `migration_ready`. Never provide stack traces, connection strings, directory listings, wallet account data or token values to users. Keep full privileged maintenance tests CLI-only and separate.

### 18.3 HTTP 404 on CLI preflight is intentional

The path checker contains a guard similar to:

```php
if (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli') {
    http_response_code(404);
    exit;
}
```

Place it in a private local/server directory and run it through `php` on the command line. Do not upload CLI maintenance scripts into a public site because you expect a browser UI.
## 19. End-user and security testing matrix

### 19.1 Before testing

Use an isolated testnet staging site, two disposable tenants, two distinct HTTPS dApp origins, separate verified test accounts, a supported mobile browser and small testnet balances controlled by the tester. Record the wallet/module versions, selected network, browser/device, test time and pass/fail observations. Never collect users' passwords, PINs, recovery phrases or private keys as test evidence.

### 19.2 Administrator tests (actual page-level steps)

1. Sign in as platform administrator to the dedicated wallet hostname.
2. Open `diagnostic-upgrade.php` and confirm host prerequisites, protected private path, existing lock and Mainnet emergency stop.
3. Open `diagnostic-tenant-v090.php`; expect `SCHEMA_AND_MAPPING_READY` without any origin ownership conflicts.
4. Open `diagnostic-phase-b-v090.php`; on staging only, expect `ENABLED_LIVE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED` once activated.
5. Open `tenants.php`; create two disposable organizations with different slugs and owners.
6. Register **different** HTTPS dApp origins for each, first with `address.read` only.
7. Test the owner, admin and viewer controls using distinct logins. Viewers must not be able to create apps or suspend others.
8. Attempt to register the same origin under the other tenant. Expect HTTP 409 with friendly guidance and no new record.
9. Attempt a dApp origin containing a path such as `https://wallet.example.org/demo`; expect immediate rejection.
10. Disable one app and then suspend one tenant. New tokens, sign requests and checkout preparation for the suspended tenant must be denied while the other tenant works.
11. Resume the tenant. Ensure its individual apps are **not automatically reactivated** and revoked tokens stay invalid.
12. Verify that historical payment status can still be audited by authorized operators.

### 19.3 Customer connection and authorization tests

1. Open a registered test merchant on desktop and click **Connect Wallet**.
2. Verify the browser is redirected to the expected wallet hostname (not an unexpected lookalike).
3. Check that the wallet shows the exact app name, origin, requested scope and network.
4. Reject connection; confirm no credential/account data is returned.
5. Repeat and approve only `address.read`; confirm the exact callback page resumes a valid session.
6. Try a wrong callback/redirect, missing `state`, invalid/reused PKCE verifier or disabled client: reject.
7. Revoke the app in the wallet and confirm the previously issued bearer token no longer works.
8. Request `tx.request` from a read-only client and confirm it is denied before any signing UI.
9. Attempt to change `tenant_id` or `client_id` in a user-controlled request. Confirm server access is based on authenticated ownership, not the changed identifier.

### 19.4 Native chain balance tests

For each enabled chain, select the exact network, enter a public testnet address and compare the returned balance with an independent explorer/provider. Check an empty/unfunded address, a funded address, a malformed address, a wrong-network address and a temporarily unavailable RPC.

| Network | End-user observation to verify |
|---|---|
| BTC Testnet4 | Correct testnet identity; satoshi count and unconfirmed movement distinguished |
| Dogecoin Testnet | Provider health is meaningful; koinu count and timeouts handled clearly |
| Solana Devnet | Lamports and SOL display match Devnet account; RPC authorization errors are specific |
| XRP Testnet | Drops and XRP display; unactivated account is stated, not disguised as RPC failure |
| BNB Testnet | RPC returns chain ID 97 and accurate wei/BNB balance |
| ETC Mordor | RPC returns chain ID 63 and accurate native balance; wrong-chain replies rejected |
| TRON Nile | Correct sun/TRX amount; unactivated Nile account distinguished from bad provider |
| Midnight | Health-only indexer checks; optional Lace consent/read works only with compatible injected wallet |
| Ethereum Sepolia | Existing confirmed R3 history remains visible; a new send is optional and separately approved |
| Cardano Preprod | Linked address and v0.8 assets match a trusted Preprod explorer; no unintended mint/sign |

A zero balance is a valid result for an unfunded address. It does not prove the funded-address parsing path is correct; at least one controlled funded account should be tested per supported balance reader before operational sign-off.

### 19.5 Wallet send/sign test (testnet only, supported chains)

1. Use a fresh small **testnet** amount; confirm the receiving address belongs to the test team.
2. Confirm chain, recipient, asset, nonce/UTxO details, estimated network fee and total before approval.
3. Cancel and verify no transaction is sent.
4. Repeat, approve using the wallet's supported authentication factor; verify a single broadcast attempt.
5. Review hash, status and confirmation depth in the wallet and a matching testnet explorer.
6. Test intentional low balance, provider timeout and duplicate submit. Do **not** blindly repeat transactions reported as `uncertain`.
7. Verify a returned failure never shows a successful value payment; maintain status and gas-fee history.
8. Attempt Mainnet selection or prohibited token/contract operation; the emergency stop must block it.

### 19.6 Customer checkout and merchant tests

| Case | Required outcome |
|---|---|
| New order creates one intent | One stable, uniquely identifiable prepared order |
| Exact retry with same order details | Idempotent result; no duplicated payable request |
| Same order ID with changed amount/recipient | Conflict/rejection |
| Suspended tenant submits checkout | Denied |
| Disabled merchant submits checkout | Denied |
| Customer cancels signing | No payment success |
| Browser callback says success but RPC cannot confirm | Remains pending/uncertain |
| Confirmed blockchain payment | Exact amount/recipient/network and policy depth verified |
| Chain revert or rejected transfer | Failure with fee implications retained |
| Reorg/provider disagreement | Pending/reconciliation state retained until policy satisfied |
| Already submitted tx with RPC timeout | Search by known hash/order before trying any retry |

### 19.7 Android and iPhone tests

Test at least Android Chrome and iOS Safari using normal phone-browser settings, not only desktop responsive emulation.

- Install no extensions for the core extension-free path; confirm the browser wallet vault can load, lock, unlock and recover using its supported factors.
- Test fresh private browsing and a new device. Browser storage may be cleared or partitioned; a user's on-chain wallet must still have a secure recovery path.
- Test multiple tabs, backgrounding, interrupted redirects, cookies, lost connectivity, screen lock, reload and returning from another app.
- Check exact content in the transaction review at narrow viewport sizes; the primary confirm/cancel controls must remain visible and unambiguous.
- Test with slow network and low device battery, plus a wallet session expiry during checkout.
- If using passkeys/WebAuthn PRF, verify actual device support, user verification and recovery fallbacks; API availability alone is insufficient evidence of hardware-backed protection.
- Confirm Midnight Lace/extension-dependent operations are accurately shown as unavailable on unsupported ordinary phone browsers.

### 19.8 Negative security tests and pass/fail criteria

1. Same-origin wallet dApp registration: rejected.
2. Cross-tenant origin claim: rejected.
3. Cross-tenant access by altered object IDs: rejected in every API route.
4. Unauthorized POST or invalid CSRF token: rejected.
5. Expired/reused OAuth authorization code: rejected.
6. Disabled tenant/app bearer token: rejected.
7. Unsigned/forged merchant request or changed immutable payment fields: rejected.
8. Browser requests arbitrary RPC URL, mainnet host or SSRF target: rejected.
9. Wrong chain ID returned by an upstream provider: rejected.
10. Outdated R3 gas estimate for a new Sepolia address: blocked, never silently sent at unsafe limit.
11. Access to `settings.php`, SQL setup data, recovery helpers and setup payload over HTTP: refused.
12. Mainnet spending without completed release gates: refused.

Record only the status code, reproducible steps, sanitized request shape, correlation ID and expected/actual outcome. Never upload bearer tokens or session cookie contents.

## 20. Security architecture and threat controls

### 20.1 Security is a system, not a PIN field

A secure wallet requires origin isolation, authenticated sessions, cryptographic key separation, transaction intent integrity, user approval, malicious RPC handling, tenant object authorization, incident response and recovery. PIN length alone cannot compensate for compromised wallet-origin JavaScript or stolen browser storage.

### 20.2 Threats and required controls

| Threat | Required treatment |
|---|---|
| Malicious merchant JavaScript | Different wallet origin, restricted SDK scopes and exact redirects |
| Wallet-origin XSS/supply-chain compromise | Strict CSP, dependency pinning, output encoding, code review, integrity and no untrusted scripts on signing pages |
| Tenant IDOR/BOLA | Server-side per-object ownership check even when `tenant_id` is supplied in request |
| CSRF and cross-origin confusion | CSRF protections, exact origin/callback, PKCE S256, state and session binding |
| Session theft/replay | Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies where appropriate; token expiry/rotation, revocation and device/session limits |
| Weak PIN/browser storage | Strong key derivation, device factors where supported, rate limiting, secure recovery and tested fallback; no unsupported "hardware-backed" claim |
| Key exfiltration | Customer signing keys stay in device-side protected wallet context or externally audited wallet; no admin APIs for exporting them |
| Malicious RPC provider | Chain/genesis identity verification, bounded responses, independent confirmation checks and fixed operator allowlists |
| Server-side request forgery | No user-controlled upstream URL, DNS/IP safety checks, TLS validation and redirect policy |
| Intent manipulation | Canonical amount, recipient, chain, nonce, expiry and fee bound to user review and merchant record |
| Duplicate spending | Idempotent requests, state machine, nonce/UTxO control and reconciliation before resubmission |
| Installed-code tampering | Signed release manifest, canonical hash comparison, permission control and independent update-verification key |
| Installer takeover | Ownership proof, one-time lock, setup cleanup and no public reinstall route |
| Tenant suspension races | Transactional policy checks, revalidation at issuance/use, immediate revocation of pending authorizations |
| Mainnet policy bypass | Global stop and per-chain gates enforced server-side and client-side; fail closed on unknown route |

### 20.3 Separation of secrets and operator data

- **User signing keys/recovery material:** Never printed in diagnostics, sent to the merchant server or administered by the platform tenant screen.
- **Server configuration:** Lives outside public roots. It may hold operator-controlled API/provider identifiers and service credentials; it must not be copied into public release bundles.
- **Public metadata:** Client IDs, public receiving addresses and non-secret chain identifiers can be shown where appropriate.
- **Payment and tenant data:** Enforce least privilege and purpose-limited retention; sensitive personal information should not appear in generic diagnostics.
- **Updater signing key:** Kept offline or in controlled build infrastructure; deployed instances only need pinned, independently trusted verification material.

### 20.4 Browser security headers and CSP

Use HTTPS/HSTS, sensible Referrer-Policy, `nosniff`, modern frame controls, cookies marked Secure and HttpOnly where applicable, and a strict Content Security Policy that permits only reviewed wallet assets. Do not blindly copy a CSP snippet if it breaks module loading; test actual `script-src`, `connect-src`, `form-action` and browser redirect behavior under strict settings. Avoid inline third-party widgets on pages where wallet credentials or transaction approval occur.

### 20.5 Wallet recovery and migrations

A browser IndexedDB vault is tied to that browser context and origin; a move from `example.org/coxy-wallet` to `wallet.example.org` changes the origin. Do **not** assume browser vault ciphertext can simply be copied across origins and unlocked. A migration needs a reviewed user-controlled recovery path and explicit verification of recovered public addresses. Never turn a recovery screen into a server-side mnemonic collection endpoint.

Passkeys and device-bound keys require platform-specific usability testing. WebAuthn PRF or nonexportable browser keys are not automatically equivalent to independently verified secure-element custody. Keep meaningful recovery and strong unlock alternatives while identifying their tradeoffs.

### 20.6 Auditing before Mainnet

At minimum, commission reviews of the browser vault crypto design, wallet/dApp origin boundary, OAuth/PKCE implementation, transaction signing, chain-specific replay protections, smart-contract/CBOR parsers, merchant reconciliation, update system, installer and tenant isolation. The review must be tied to the exact released build hash and threats being audited. A passed unit suite alone does not satisfy this requirement.

Useful benchmarks: OWASP ASVS 5.0, OWASP API Security Top 10, IETF RFC 9700 and the relevant blockchain wallet/contract standards.

## 21. Logging, incident handling, backup and recovery

### 21.1 A structured audit event

Store an authorized, minimally identifying record for sensitive operations:

```json
{
  "event_type": "merchant.intent.state_changed",
  "event_time_utc": "2026-10-19T10:00:00Z",
  "correlation_id": "example-correlation-id",
  "tenant_ref": "opaque-tenant-reference",
  "app_ref": "opaque-app-reference",
  "network": "testnet-identifier",
  "old_state": "submitted",
  "new_state": "confirmed",
  "reason_code": "CHAIN_RECEIPT_VALIDATED",
  "actor_role": "system-reconciler"
}
```

This is an **illustrative structure**, not an asserted existing JSON schema. Do not log passwords, access tokens, signed bearer headers, PINs, private wallet keys, recovery phrases, full private addresses or provider credentials. Access logs and audit logs require independent access controls and explicit retention/erasure policies.

### 21.2 Incident playbooks

**Provider outage:** Distinguish timeout, 429, 403, 404 and invalid response; keep last verified state; do not silently switch to an unverified Mainnet or wrong-testnet provider.

**Transaction uncertain:** Capture known hash and intent fingerprint. Reconcile via trusted same-network providers. Do not blindly resend. Preserve submitted-at time, nonce/UTxO reference and explorer evidence.

**Tenant compromise:** Suspend tenant and affected apps, revoke permissions/tokens, block new checkout/signing, preserve authorized audit evidence and notify relevant operators. Continue read-only investigation of already-broadcast funds.

**Wallet-origin compromise:** Stop new signing, isolate compromised deployment, retain snapshots/logs, investigate released asset integrity and affected credentials, rotate operator-side infrastructure credentials where appropriate, and guide users through a controlled recovery procedure. Never ask them to send recovery words to support.

**Bad upgrade:** Disable new writes/signing where practical, restore a coherent source/database snapshot, preserve transaction reconciliation, compare checksums, and test remediation before reactivating.

### 21.3 Backups are not the same as user wallet recovery

Maintain separate protected backups of: application source, release manifest, configuration templates, operator-controlled private configuration, SQL database and allowed audit logs. A website backup is not guaranteed to restore browser-side self-custody material. User-owned recovery material must have its own secure, private recovery lifecycle.

Suggested operational cadence (adjust by risk and retention law): encrypted daily relational backups, off-host copies, weekly restore drills on sanitized test data, release-time snapshots, and periodic incident-response exercises. Encrypt backups and restrict access; do not store them under webroot or in public source repositories.

### 21.4 Observability on cheap/shared hosting

Where persistent workers are unavailable, use bounded cron-triggered reconciliation and a small queue table rather than unbounded requests within page loads. Limit outbound requests per transaction, cache non-sensitive network metadata, apply exponential backoff for rate limits and publish aggregate error rates without exposing customer details. PHP-FPM/opcache, indexed database queries and HTTP timeout limits help reliability, but cannot compensate for an unreliable external RPC.

## 22. How to add a new blockchain or framework adapter

### 22.1 Chain adapter interface (recommended development contract)

```text
adapter.id
adapter.networks
adapter.capabilities            (e.g. balance.read, tx.request)
adapter.validatePublicAddress()
adapter.getNetworkIdentity()
adapter.getBalance()
adapter.getTransactionStatus()
adapter.getExplorerLink()
adapter.buildUnsignedRequest()   (only when reviewed)
adapter.signOnUserDevice()       (separately audited)
adapter.broadcastSignedTx()     (separately audited)
```

This is a **proposed interface contract**, not a claim that existing PHP/JS classes use these exact method names. Keep chain adapters isolated behind the same parent authentication and authorization rules.

### 22.2 Step-by-step network addition

1. Identify exact network, address format, native units, supported assets, faucet and explorer.
2. Create read-only metadata and an allowlisted provider health probe.
3. Add PHP request schema validation, timeout, rate limits and network identity checks.
4. Add browser adapter/read-only page with canonical amount formatting.
5. Add negative tests: bad address, wrong network, empty account, huge integer, RPC 404/429/500, malformed JSON, DNS/TLS timeout and provider wrong-chain response.
6. Add operator-only feature flag and diagnostics; keep signing disabled.
7. Prove a public funded balance independently on a matching testnet explorer.
8. Design signing only after network-specific transaction review, fee logic, key custody, replay protection and audit tests pass.
9. Keep Mainnet separately disabled until independent review.

### 22.3 Adapting an existing website or application framework

A platform-specific plugin/adapter should implement four layers:

- **Site configuration:** Exact HTTPS origin and public client ID, but no wallet secrets in the browser.
- **User bridge:** User-initiated Connect/Pay button and exact callback/PKCE handling.
- **Server payment bridge:** Idempotent intent creation, business-specific order mapping and verified settlement polling.
- **Administration/audit:** Merchant configuration, reconciliation status, refunds, error handling and tenant-scoped roles.

Possible deliverables: WordPress/WooCommerce gateway; Laravel package; plain PHP include/SDK; Magento/PrestaShop integration; Django/Rails/Node frontend SDK with a tested backend HTTP payment client. Each is an independent compatibility and security test matrix; avoid advertising official support before completion.

### 22.4 Multi-tenant API correctness

All SQL data access should check the application's authenticated tenant identity and the object's actual ownership. Do not rely on predictable integer IDs or browser-supplied `tenant_id`. Test requests where Tenant A uses Tenant B's valid client ID, origin, payment ID, order ID and wallet link. Require denial even if the SQL row exists.


## 23. Frequent installation and integration failures

| Symptom / status | Meaning | Safe next action |
|---|---|---|
| "Coxy Wallet is not installed" | Loader cannot locate expected private bootstrap or installer lock | Check real document root and private sibling files; do **not** reinstall |
| "Wallet public path does not match private installation settings" | `wallet_base_path` points to a filesystem directory or wrong URL | For dedicated root use `''`; for folder mode use public path `/coxy-wallet` |
| CLI preflight returns browser HTTP 404 | Intentionally CLI-only | Move file outside public_html and run with `php` in CLI |
| `assets/tenant-v090.mjs` returns HTTP 404 | Module not deployed at public URL | Map ZIP folder-style path to actual dedicated-root asset directory |
| `Loading tenant permissions...` forever | Frontend module missing, MIME/CSP problem or API initialization error | Check Network status + MIME + Console; do not change database |
| `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'reset')` | Previous frontend awaited event handler then read `currentTarget` | Use Phase B reset/R2 hotfix; check whether tenant was already saved |
| `INVALID_DAPP_REGISTRATION` HTTP 422 | Bad name, origin or callback | Use full origin-only HTTPS URL and full same-origin callback |
| `ORIGIN_ALREADY_ASSIGNED` HTTP 409 | Origin claimed by another tenant | Use a new independent dApp origin; do not delete claim blindly |
| `POST_REQUIRED` opening API in browser | Route requires authenticated POST | Submit through authorized UI, not URL bar |
| `DAPP_WALLET_ORIGIN_FORBIDDEN` | dApp origin equals wallet hostname | Use separate dApp HTTPS origin and document root |
| BNB JSON-RPC URL shows 404 on browser GET | JSON-RPC endpoint is not a web page; or invalid path | Use a bounded POST health method, exact network chain ID 97 |
| ETC invalid balance/identity | Mordor RPC method unsupported, malformed reply or wrong chain | Check address-free RPC health, require chain ID 63 |
| TRON Nile 0 account not activated | Address exists in valid format but no funded account state | Activate using testnet faucet only if user owns account |
| Midnight missing Lace | No compatible injected wallet/permission | Network health-only mode; don't simulate private balance |
| Provider HTTP 403/429/timeout | Upstream block/quota/connectivity | Diagnose outbound host/provider; never disable TLS or url checks |
| Sepolia full gas limit/reverted | Earlier fixed gas limits, provider/state or transaction failure | Dynamic estimate, exact review, receipt forensics; no blind resend |
| v0.8 flag `INTENTIONALLY_DISABLED` | Optional feature off by design | Test prerequisites, then activate selectively on staging |
| `READY_TO_ACTIVATE_ON_STAGING` | Phase B hooks installed, not active | Back up and enable only in isolated staging |
| `ENABLED_LIVE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED` | Phase B flag on, real checks pending | Run two-tenant + checkout acceptance suite |
| `AUDIT_NOT_SUPPLIED` | Independent audit evidence absent | Keep Mainnet disabled; commission reviewed release-specific audit |
| Signed updater `DISABLED` | Expected staging safety gate | Do not activate without verified signatures, rollback and path rules |

**Response discipline:** An error that mentions one network or API does not authorize changing unrelated working signing modules. Use small targeted diffs, unit tests, reproducible diagnostic codes and rollback instructions.

## 24. Product advantages, limitations and future roadmap

### 24.1 Advantages if release gates are met

1. **Self-hosted operator control:** Website owners choose the hosting account, database and allowed chain infrastructure, subject to secure deployment requirements.
2. **Shared-hosting access:** Does not inherently require each merchant to deploy an always-on blockchain node or extension-specific wallet plugin.
3. **Common SDK:** One wallet connection and permission vocabulary for multiple independent applications.
4. **Multichain modularity:** Chain-specific parsing and providers can be reviewed independently.
5. **Tenant governance:** Independent organizations, roles, origin claims, suspension and audit logs.
6. **Merchant integration:** An order/intent/reconciliation framework avoids ad hoc address-only payment flows.
7. **Browser-first experience:** Supported customer actions can work on ordinary HTTPS-capable phone browsers without a desktop extension.
8. **Recoverability and operator diagnostics:** Installation, tenant and chain diagnostics make failures explainable rather than silently retrying financial operations.
9. **Incremental adoption:** Websites may begin with public balances and read-only connections before enabling sensitive capabilities.

### 24.2 Limitations that must be stated publicly

- Self-hosting does **not** automatically make any operator or client code trustworthy.
- A compromised wallet-origin JavaScript bundle can endanger browser-side signing safety.
- RPC endpoints, block explorers and external nodes can fail or misreport; independent verification matters.
- The current v0.7 additional adapters are often read-only, not complete sending wallets.
- Midnight Lace is currently not a generally extension-free mobile workflow.
- Mainnet, audited custody, universal token support, refunds and regulated payment operations remain gated.
- Browser recovery and WebAuthn availability vary by phone, device lock policy and authenticator.
- A Softaculous-compatible custom package still needs host-level installation and acceptance; it is not yet in the public installer catalog.

### 24.3 Recommended next engineering milestones

